1. the two defaults
   Plain.new.to_s starts with "#<Plain:0x"  true
   Plain.new.inspect == Plain.new.to_s (shape) true
   Point.new(1, 2).inspect ends " @x=1, @y=2>" true
   Point.new(1, 2).to_s includes "@x"       false
2. defining one never defines the other
   OnlyToS.new.to_s                         only to_s
   OnlyToS.new.inspect is still the default true
   OnlyInspect.new.inspect                  <OnlyInspect>
   OnlyInspect.new.to_s is still the default true
3. who calls which
   puts obj                                 to_s of 3
   p obj                                    #<Both 3>
   "#{obj}"                                 to_s of 3
   format("%s / %p", obj, obj)              to_s of 3 / #<Both 3>
   String(obj)                              to_s of 3
   obj.to_s                                 to_s of 3
   obj.inspect                              #<Both 3>
4. containers inspect their elements, even in to_s
   [obj].to_s                               [#<Both 3>]
   [obj].inspect                            [#<Both 3>]
   { k: obj }.to_s                          {k: #<Both 3>}
   "#{[obj]}"                               [#<Both 3>]
   puts [obj]                               to_s of 3
5. p returns its argument, puts returns nil
   p(obj) prints: #<Both 3>
     ...and returns obj itself              true
   p(1, 2) prints two lines:
1
2
     ...and returns                         [1, 2]
   p() returns                              nil
   puts("one") prints: one
     ...and returns                         nil
6. nil
   puts nil writes                          "\n"
   p nil writes                             "nil\n"
   print nil writes                         ""
   "#{nil}".inspect                         ""
   nil.to_s.inspect                         ""
   nil.inspect                              nil
   [nil].to_s                               [nil]
7. strings and symbols
   "str".to_s                               str
   "str".inspect                            "str"
   "tab\there".inspect                      "tab\there"
   "\u{e9}".inspect                         "é"
   :sym.to_s                                sym
   :sym.inspect                             :sym
8. core classes define both
   S.new(1, 2).inspect                      #<struct S x=1, y=2>
   S.new(1, 2).to_s == its inspect          true
   D.new(1).inspect                         #<data D a=1>
   RuntimeError.new("boom").to_s            boom
   RuntimeError.new("boom").inspect         #<RuntimeError: boom>
   1e20.to_s                                1.0e+20
   (1..3).inspect                           1..3
9. pp wraps a nested structure at a width
{name: "widget",
 parts:
  [{id: 1,
    tags: ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"]},
   {id: 2,
    tags: ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"]},
   {id: 3,
    tags: ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"]}]}
   obj.pretty_inspect == obj.inspect + "\n" true
10. where they live
   Object.instance_method(:to_s).owner      Kernel
   Object.instance_method(:inspect).owner   Kernel
   Kernel.private_instance_methods.include?(:p) true
   BasicObject.instance_methods.include?(:inspect) false
